  "final_span": "to the other 500 global Caterham teams who are competing with Caterham karts in races around the world.\n\"It\u2019s not about prices; it\u2019s not even about anything tangible. It\u2019s just about everyone being connected online, and enabling location independence,\" says Peters.\nTruphone\u2019s sponsorship deal with Caterham differs from others\u2019 sponsorships. In addition to offering communications assistance, Truphone supplies financial access to its offerings to Caterham\u2019s workers, as stated by David Powers, global partnerships director at Truphone.\n\"It\u2019s to enable staff anywhere in the world to have complete cost-effective access to their personal communications and data services solutions, and if they get charged right in a country... they\u2019re still going to pay less,\" says Powers.\n\"Caterham is the only hospitality organisation that is fully global and it gives them a fully global audience to deploy local technology to,\" says Powers.\n\"It\u2019s very much of an education process, and a process of explaining honestly and openly where the F1 season is taking them,\" he says.\nOne potential application of the Truphone service is to track Caterham\u2019s F1 racers, with Abiteboul calculating that it could be worth approximately \u00a31m to him. Previously, most vehicle data was typically kept in one place.\n\"For 50,000 people tuning in and wanting to know what\u2019s going, they will probably get different answers from the [F1] drivers, their manager, their engineers,\" Abiteboul said.\n\"[We] mobilise the car and the technology to war as much as to race,\" says Abiteboul.\nDuring the ongoing Formula One season, Aeris offers cloud and server-based Live Timing services accessible on all devices, including smartphones and tablets.\nChoosing the appropriate technology was crucial for Caterham in aligning costs with benefits while ensuring data is effectively managed during transit.\nJohn Marsh, Caterham's global head of applications, came on board to ensure that the IT systems stayed compliant with Formula One regulations and functioning as needed, with provisioning becoming his top priority from the outset.\n\"It",
